      "writtenOn": "2026-09-10T17:54:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Storing the PID is error prone, as PIDs can be reused. Also, I believe different threads--even if they belong to the same process--have different PIDs, at least from looking at `kernel/fork.c::copy_process()`.\n\nCould we use the `group_leader` as we were doing in C?",
      "revId": "627ecef83bfad9096784006ff893aa1c839da082",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3a8c8a26_fb9e927c",
        "filename": "drivers/android/wrapfd/wrapped_device.rs",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 44,
      "author": {
        "id": 1014088
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-09-11T14:07:22Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I need to look into this, I did so before and things in the rust api were not terribly obvious to me, i\u0027ll have another look. I was under the impression that we wanted this to assume ownershop on a per-thread basis, not per-process, is that not true? As for PIDs being reused, could this actually be a problem here? If a process dies it clearly takes the reference on the driver with it, right? If that used to think that that pid owned the driver for the time being it would no longer be an issue, or am I misunderstanding?",
      "parentUuid": "cc2e16f9_e98dd217",
      "revId": "627ecef83bfad9096784006ff893aa1c839da082",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2b9dda27_140da1d0",
        "filename": "drivers/android/wrapfd/wrapped_device.rs",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 44,
      "author": {
        "id": 1934715
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-09-11T16:23:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e I was under the impression that we wanted this to assume ownershop on a per-thread basis, not per-process, is that not true?\n\nNo. The concept of ownership is meant to be per-process. An application can be made up of multiple threads that might want to operate on the buffer, and having ownership be a thread-level mechanism would be restrictive for that.\n\nWith regard to the PID being reused, thinking about it more, I think you\u0027re right. I need to think about this more; specifically the case where a process has a reference to a wrapfd, and then gives the wrapfd to another driver. The driver can take a reference, the process can die, and at that point, it\u0027s not clear to me what happens since the wrap is still alive because of the driver reference, but its owner is gone.\n\nIf the PID gets reused in that scenario, the new process would have to get an fd to the wrap, which I think could happen if it did share the wrap with other processes and it looks through `/proc/$other_proc_pid/fd`?",
      "parentUuid": "3a8c8a26_fb9e927c",
      "revId": "627ecef83bfad9096784006ff893aa1c839da082",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
